Use a Dolby Vision scene's L0 peak when its L1 peak is too dark.

Some Dolby Vision scenes carry an L1 max so low that tone mapping turns the
whole scene black, though it has visible detail. Fall back to the L0 peak
when L1 drops below a meaningful value, and to 1000 nits when neither is
believable. This covers the CUDA, OpenCL, D3D11, VideoToolbox and tonemapx
filters.

Taken from jellyfin-ffmpeg 7c1a6e01 (gnattu), on the feat/dovi-fel-tonemappers
branch behind PR #774, where it sits under the unmerged enhancement-layer
work. It does not depend on that work. Drop this once upstream carries it.

Index: FFmpeg/libavfilter/colorspace.c
===================================================================
--- FFmpeg.orig/libavfilter/colorspace.c
+++ FFmpeg/libavfilter/colorspace.c
@@ -232,6 +232,21 @@
     return peak;
 }
 
+double ff_determine_dovi_tonemap_peak(AVFrame *in, const AVDOVIMetadata *data,
+                                     int l0_only)
+{
+    double peak = ff_determine_dovi_signal_peak(data, l0_only);
+
+    if (!l0_only && peak <= 1.0)
+        peak = ff_determine_dovi_signal_peak(data, 1);
+    if (peak <= 1.0)
+        peak = ff_determine_signal_peak(in);
+    if (peak <= 1.0)
+        peak = 10.0;
+
+    return peak;
+}
+
